2. THE CASE HAS NO AUTHORED TEXT -- what was designed, and from what
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Read from ck.db on tb277 before dispatch: AWPTCM-T33401 has `has_objective = 0` and ONE
step whose text is the 53-character empty-JSON placeholder. There is no objective, no
step text and no expected result in the database. Folder
"/New Platform Test (MASTER)/New Platform Template/Management", status Draft,
script_type STEP_BY_STEP, label New_Platform_Management.

So the case was designed, before it was run, from the three sources STANDING-ORDERS
allows, in this order:

  1. THE TITLE: "Management_Operation - Syslog Facility Override". The subject is the
     facility field of the syslog messages the DUT sends, and the ability to OVERRIDE it.
  2. THE X960 TPS ROW. The bracketed "(266)" in the title is NOT the TPS row number --
     on this suite the title brackets are offset +2. The row for this case is
     **264, "Operation / Syslog facility override (Log)", Supported = Yes**. Because the
     TPS says Yes, a feature missing on the DUT would be a FINDING, not a bench limit.
  3. THE DUT'S OWN CLI SURFACE, read read-only with tools/qmark.py before designing --
     see section 3 below, which is where the first finding of this case came from.

WHAT "FACILITY" IS, AND WHY THE VERDICT CANNOT REST ON `show log config`
  A syslog message begins with one number in angle brackets, the PRI:

      PRI = facility * 8 + severity                       (RFC 3164 / RFC 5424)

  so "the facility was overridden" is a claim about ONE NUMBER THAT ARRIVED AT THE
  COLLECTOR. `show log config` prints the device's own intention; it is the same class of
  evidence as `show mirror` reading "Enabled" while the dataplane copied nothing
  (platforms/x960.md §5). This case is therefore graded on the received PRI, decoded from
  a bounded tcpdump capture by tools/syslogwire.py, and `show log config` is recorded
  beside it only so the two can be compared.

OBJECTIVE AS DESIGNED
  Prove that the AT-x960-56YSQ sends syslog messages to a remote collector with the
  default facility when none is configured; that `log facility <f>` overrides that
  facility ON THE WIRE for every destination; that the per-destination
  `log host <ip> facility <f>` overrides the global setting for THAT destination ONLY,
  measured against a second destination in the SAME capture window; that the severity
  field is untouched by either override; that a configured but unreachable destination
  draws nothing; that the override survives a cold boot; and that removing it returns the
  wire to the default facility and then to silence.

DESIGNED STEPS, WRITTEN DOWN BEFORE THEY WERE RUN
  STEP 0  Baseline OFF, on the wire. With no `log host` and no `log facility` configured,
          a bounded capture on the collector leg must contain ZERO syslog packets, while
          the same capture shows live ICMP so the instrument is proved to be looking at
          the right wire.
  STEP 1  Remote logging ON, facility NOT overridden. Configure the real collector and,
          in the same breath, an unreachable CONTROL destination on the same /27. A
          marker message must arrive with the DEFAULT facility; the control destination
          must draw zero packets in the same window.
  STEP 2  The GLOBAL override. `log facility local4`. The same marker must now arrive
          with facility local4 at BOTH real destinations, and no message in that window
          may carry the default facility.
  STEP 3  The PER-DESTINATION override, measured against the global one in ONE window.
          `log host 10.38.22.33 facility local5` while the global stays local4, with both
          legs captured simultaneously: .33 must read local5 and .65 must read local4,
          for the same messages at the same moment.
  STEP 4  Severity is NOT collateral. A deliberately failed command emits an error-level
          message in the same window as the notice-level markers; both must carry the
          overridden facility and their own, different, severities.
  STEP 5  Persistence. `write` + `reload`, and with NOTHING typed to reconfigure, the
          overrides must still be in force on the wire after a cold boot.
  STEP 6  Removal. `no log facility` returns the wire to the default facility;
          `no log host ...` returns it to silence; and both running- and startup-config
          must be clean afterwards, proved by re-reading, not by the absence of an error.

