>>> READING R-1, taken from the help text BEFORE anything was configured -- and then
>>> CORRECTED BY MEASUREMENT in step 3. It is left here with its correction because the
>>> first reading is the one a person would naturally take from this help output.
>>>
>>> WHAT THE HELP SHOWS. The two commands that both contain the word `facility` do NOT
>>> offer the same facilities:
>>>   global          `log facility ?`                 -> 20 facilities
>>>   per-destination `log host <ip> facility ?`       -> 13 facilities
>>> The seven the second does NOT offer are local0, local1, local2, local3, local4,
>>> local6 and local7, and the one local facility it does offer is described differently
>>> by the two commands:
>>>   global          local5  "local use 5"
>>>   per-destination local5  "UTM feature messages"
>>> `log host 10.38.22.33 facility local1` is rejected outright: "% Unrecognized command".
>>>
>>> THE READING TAKEN AT DESIGN TIME (and it was WRONG): that both commands override the
>>> facility, and that the per-destination one can only override to thirteen of the
>>> twenty values -- an asymmetry worth reporting. The case was designed to use local4
>>> globally and local5 per-destination precisely because that pair is distinguishable on
>>> the wire.
>>>
>>> WHAT STEP 3 MEASURED, AND WHAT IT MEANS: see FINDING F-1 in section 8. The two
>>> commands are not two forms of one feature at all. `log host <ip> facility <f>` adds a
>>> message-SELECTION FILTER, not an override, and the thirteen-item list is therefore
>>> not a restricted set of override targets -- it is the set of facilities an AW+
>>> subsystem can actually RAISE a message under. Read that way the two lists are both
>>> correct and the "asymmetry" dissolves. The design above still stands, because it is
>>> exactly the configuration that exposed the difference.

THE STIMULUS THIS CASE USES, AND WHY IT NEEDS NO TRAFFIC AND NO PORT FLAP
  Reading `show log tail 30` before any configuration showed that the DUT logs EVERY CLI
  command that is typed:

    2026 Oct 21 23:54:25 user.notice awplus IMISH[4271]: [manager@ttyS0]show clock
    2026 Oct 21 23:54:26 user.notice awplus IMISH[4271]: [manager@ttyS0]show system
    ...
    2026 Oct 21 23:53:10 user.err    awplus IMISH[4271]: Command [end] failed

  so typing a harmless command that carries a unique marker string produces, on demand,
  a syslog message with KNOWN TEXT, KNOWN FACILITY (`user`, 1) and KNOWN SEVERITY
  (`notice`, 5) -- PRI 13 -- and a command that is valid but fails in the current mode
  (`end` at the exec prompt) produces one at `user.err`, severity 3, in the same window.
  That is the whole stimulus. No traffic generator, no port flap and no second switch.

