  passes each message out under the facility of the subsystem that raised it -- `user`
  for the CLI's IMISH messages, `syslog` for syslog-ng's own. That MIXTURE is the precise
  thing an override has to collapse, and it is what makes the next two steps measurable.

  THE CONTROL, in the same capture, on the same wire, in the same window:
    tools/tb tool ... syslogwire /tmp/oper1022/s1e-eth3.pcap --to 10.38.22.40 --quiet
      12 frames in the capture, 0 syslog message(s) on port(s) 514
      NO SYSLOG MESSAGES. For a control leg that is the measurement, not an error.  (exit 2)
  10.38.22.40 was configured identically to 10.38.22.33 -- same `log host`, same
  `level notices` -- and is an address on vlan10's own /27 that nothing answers. It drew
  ZERO packets while the real destination drew twelve.

  Two further legs were captured in the same window and were silent, which locates the
  traffic as well as counting it:
    eth2 (the box's leg to swi_d's out-of-band eth0):  1 frame, 0 syslog
    eth4 (vlan48):                                     0 frames, 0 syslog
  -- correct: at this point only 10.38.22.33 was a working destination, and vlan10 is the
  only route to it.

  STEP 1 VERDICT: PASS -- remote syslog works, the default facility is the ORIGINATING
  facility of each message (user and syslog seen), and an identically configured
  unreachable destination drew nothing.

