8. STEP 3 -- THE PER-DESTINATION "facility" COMMAND. FINDING F-1.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DESIGNED: `log host 10.38.22.33 facility local5` while the global stays local4, with both
legs captured simultaneously; .33 should read local5 and .65 local4, for the same
messages at the same moment. That design assumed both commands were overrides. They are
not, and three windows were needed to establish what the second one really is.

8a. The configuration is accepted and appears in the running-config

  awplus# configure terminal
  awplus(config)# log host 10.38.22.33 facility local5
  awplus(config)# end
  awplus# show running-config | include log
  log facility local4
  log host 10.38.22.33
  log host 10.38.22.33 level notices
  log host 10.38.22.33 facility local5          <- the line under test
  log host 10.38.22.40
  log host 10.38.22.40 level notices
  log host 10.38.22.65
  log host 10.38.22.65 level notices

8b. WINDOW 1 (s3) -- global local4 PLUS per-host local5 on .33. 170 s, both legs.

  eth3, the destination that carries `facility local5`:
    syslogwire ... --expect-facility local5
      25 frames in the capture, 25 syslog message(s) on port(s) 514
      by facility: local4(20)=25
      by PRI:      163=local4.err x6  165=local4.notice x19
      10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.33                                   25
      EXPECT facility local5(21): 0 of 25 message(s) match  !! 25 DO NOT      (exit 4)
  eth4, the destination that does not:
      25 frames, 25 messages, by facility: local4(20)=25, 163 x6 / 165 x19
      EXPECT facility local4(20): 25 of 25 message(s) match                   (exit 0)

  So the configured line changed NOTHING on the wire. Both destinations carried the
  global facility. At this point the honest statement is only "it had no effect here",
  and two readings are still open: (i) it is an override that the global one outranks,
  or (ii) it is not an override at all.

8c. WHAT `show log config` ACTUALLY SAYS -- and it names the mechanism

  awplus# show log config
    Host 10.38.22.33 log:
      Time offset .... +0:00          Offset type .... UTC
      Source ......... -              Secured ........ disabled
      Delay parameters:   Startup-Delay: 120    Buffered-Messages: 2000
      Filters:
      1   Level ...... notices    Program .... any    Facility ... any
          Msg text ... any        Type ....... include
      2   Level ...... any        Program .... any    Facility ... local5
          Msg text ... any        Type ....... include
      Statistics ..... 2295 messages received, 168 accepted, 0 excluded

  The command did not set a property of the destination. It appended a FILTER -- filter
  2, "Level any, Program any, Facility local5, Msg text any, Type include" -- in exactly
  the same shape as the `Level notices` filter beside it. That is a rule for CHOOSING
  which messages to send, not for rewriting them.

8d. WINDOW 2 (s3b) -- the negative half. Global override REMOVED, per-host line KEPT.

  awplus(config)# no log facility                    (running-config now has no `log facility`)
  awplus# show log config | include Facility:   ->   Facility: default
  170 s, both legs, markers CKT33401-E1..E6 spread across the window.

    eth3 (.33, still carries `facility local5`):
      15 frames, 15 messages
      by facility: user(1)=9  syslog(5)=6
      by PRI:      11=user.err x1  13=user.notice x8  43=syslog.err x2  45=syslog.notice x4
    eth4 (.65, no per-host facility line at all):
      15 frames, 15 messages
      by facility: user(1)=9  syslog(5)=6
      by PRI:      11=user.err x1  13=user.notice x8  43=syslog.err x2  45=syslog.notice x4

  Byte-for-byte the same distribution at both destinations: the original, un-overridden
  mixture. With nothing outranking it, the per-host line still did not restamp a single
  message. Reading (i) is dead.

8e. WINDOW 3 (s3c) -- the POSITIVE half. What the command DOES do, proved by selection.

  If `log host <ip> facility <f>` is a selection filter, then a destination whose ONLY
  filter is `facility user` must receive the user-facility messages and NOT the
  syslog-facility ones -- while a destination filtered by level receives both. Both
  configured at once, measured in ONE 170 s window:

  awplus(config)# no log host 10.38.22.65 level notices
  awplus(config)# log host 10.38.22.65 facility user
  (running-config: `log host 10.38.22.65` + `log host 10.38.22.65 facility user`, and
   .33 unchanged with `level notices` + `facility local5`)

    eth3 (.33, Level notices + Facility local5):
      13 frames, 13 messages
      by facility: user(1)=8   syslog(5)=5
      by PRI:      11=user.err x1  13=user.notice x7  43=syslog.err x2  45=syslog.notice x3
    eth4 (.65, Facility user ONLY):
      7 frames, 7 messages
      by facility: user(1)=7
      by PRI:      11=user.err x1  13=user.notice x6
      ZERO syslog-facility messages.

  The discriminator is the complete ABSENCE of the `syslog` facility at .65 while .33
  received five of them in the same window from the same device. (The user counts, 8 and
  7, differ only by where the 60 s flush boundaries fell; the counts are not the
  measurement -- the missing facility class is.)

>>> FINDING F-1 (CORRECTS the design-time reading R-1 in section 3)
>>>
>>> ON THE AT-x960-56YSQ THERE IS NO PER-DESTINATION SYSLOG FACILITY OVERRIDE.
>>> `log host <ip> facility <f>` is a message-SELECTION FILTER on the facility a message
>>> was RAISED under. It is not an override and it never rewrites the PRI byte.
>>>   - with a global override in force, it changed nothing        (8b, 25/25 still local4)
>>>   - with no global override at all, it changed nothing         (8d, identical to a
>>>                                                                 destination without it)
>>>   - used as a filter, it selects                               (8e, 7/7 user, 0 syslog)
>>> `show log config` is honest about this -- it lists the setting under `Filters:` -- but
>>> the CLI help is not: `log host <ip> facility ?` is introduced as "Specify a syslog
>>> facility", word for word the same idea as the global `log facility ?`, and the
>>> running-config line `log host 10.38.22.33 facility local5` sitting one line below
>>> `log facility local4` reads unmistakably like a per-destination override of it.
>>>
>>> This also EXPLAINS AND DISMISSES the "asymmetry" of R-1. The global command offers all
>>> twenty RFC facilities because it may stamp any of them. The per-host command offers
>>> thirteen because those are the facilities an AW+ subsystem can actually RAISE a
>>> message under -- kern, user, mail, daemon, auth, syslog, lpr, news, uucp, cron,
>>> authpriv, ftp and local5 ("UTM feature messages"). Both lists are correct for their
>>> own command. Only reading the two as the same feature makes them look inconsistent.
>>>
>>> IMPACT: an operator who wants different facilities at different collectors cannot
>>> have them on this platform, and the configuration that looks like it does that is
>>> accepted in silence. It does not make the TPS row false -- TPS 264 "Syslog facility
>>> override" is satisfied by the global `log facility`, which works (step 2) -- so this
>>> is reported as a finding about the CLI's clarity and about a capability gap, not as
>>> a failure of the item under test.

  STEP 3 VERDICT: PASS, with FINDING F-1. The behaviour was fully characterised in three
  windows, each with its own control, rather than being left at "the command had no
  effect". Removing the global override (8d) also demonstrates the removal limb: the wire
  returned to the default facility mixture at both destinations.

