14. SUMMARY OF STEP VERDICTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  STEP 0  baseline OFF, zero syslog with the instrument proved alive ......... PASS
  STEP 1  remote logging on, DEFAULT facility measured on the wire;
          an identically configured unreachable destination drew zero ........ PASS
  STEP 2  the GLOBAL override in force at two destinations, 25/25 and 25/25
          local4, zero messages at the pre-override facility ................. PASS
  STEP 3  the per-destination `facility` command characterised in three
          windows -- FINDING F-1: it is a FILTER, not an override ............ PASS (finding)
  STEP 4  severity untouched by the override (2 severities in step 2,
          4 severities in step 5, each keeping its own value) ................ PASS
  STEP 5  persistence across a cold boot with nothing typed: 387 messages,
          all local4, over two destinations .................................. PASS
  STEP 6  removal complete in running-config, startup-config, `show log
          config` and on the wire ........................................... PASS

  FINDINGS AND OBSERVATIONS RAISED
    F-1  No per-destination syslog facility override exists on this platform.
         `log host <ip> facility <f>` is a message-selection filter, although the help
         text and the running-config read like an override. Characterised positively and
         negatively (section 8). The TPS item itself is satisfied by the global command.
    O-1  `log host <ip>` on its own is accepted, appears configured, and sends nothing
         ever: the destination has an empty filter list and a permanent `0 accepted`.
    O-2  `log host <ip> level <sev>` is refused ("% host address ... not found") unless
         `log host <ip>` was entered on a previous line, although the running-config
         presents them as two independent lines.
    O-3  BENCH MECHANISM, not a product defect: with no UDP/514 listener on the collector,
         every datagram draws an ICMP port-unreachable, the DUT's syslog-ng marks the
         destination broken and reopens it after exactly 60 s (`time_reopen='60'` in its
         own log), flushing the backlog in one sub-millisecond burst. A capture window
         shorter than ~70 s can read ZERO while the feature works perfectly. Three such
         windows occurred in this case and are reported as instrument artefacts.
    O-4  At boot the two destinations receive different message counts, because a
         destination is only written to once its egress SVI is up. Not message loss.
    M-1  The source address of a syslog datagram is the EGRESS SVI, not one fixed address.
    M-2  The flush cadence against a non-listening collector is 60.00 s, measured.

RUN LABEL: clean. (The health tool read DIRTY; the tester adjudicated CLEAN in section 11
with the reasoning set out in full. Every flagged line is the boot log of this case's own
permitted reload, matched to the second against the reload transcript.)

VERDICT: PASS -- the global syslog facility override is in force on the received PRI byte
at two destinations at once (25/25 and 25/25 local4), it leaves severity untouched, the
default facility is entirely absent while it is set, an identically configured unreachable
destination drew zero packets in every window, it survives a cold boot with nothing typed
(387/387 messages local4 across four severities), and it removes cleanly from the wire and
from both configurations. Reported with FINDING F-1: the per-destination
`log host <ip> facility` command is a message-selection filter, not an override, so
per-destination facilities cannot be set on this platform although the configuration
reads as though they can.
